rdid cells for  further experimentation  now there are some limitations to flow  cytometry  um flow cytometry can only measure the  total of signal it can't really  distinguish between the specificity of  staining of internal structures  um and sometimes this is important for  um for different phenotypes another  major con is that you lose all of the  spatial information in the tissue since  you have to disincorporate the tissue  you end up with just a mixture of cells  and you don't know which ones were near  which others and that can be a  difficulty when you're trying to  quantify the changes in the immune cell  population in terms of how close the  immune cells are to interacting with one  another or to the tumor cells  furthermore only suspended cells can be  put through a flow cytometer and as I  mentioned you have to disaggregate these  tissues using enzymes and so because of  this you can't use flow cytometry on  archived formal and fixed paraffin  embedded tissue specimens it's just not  an option now of course  a huge amount of the tissue specimens  stored in the world from former clinical  trials and research are stored as ffpe  tissue blocks and so enabling a  technique that would allow us to do this  type of immune cell population analysis  on ffpe tissues will be very valuable to  cancer research and to research in  general  So to that end we sought to develop a  technique to do exactly that we wanted  to characterize the immune cell  populations in these archival tissues to  take advantage of the millions of  archived tissue specimens that Pharma  clients  research depositories universities  research hospitals Etc have at their  disposal the most critical aspect is  that these these these stored clinical  archive samples have a huge amount of  information about you know the  progression of the patient's disease and  Etc so this represents an extremely  large repository of information about  disease that we can get access to so how  are we going to do this so the way that  we accomplished this was to adapt the  multiplex immunofluorescent techniques  that we had developed for doing High  Plex immunohistochemistry using digital  slide scanning and we developed some  open source Data Mining and  visualization tools that allowed us to  develop what we call tissue cytometry  this is essentially doing the same exact  kind of analysis where you're measuring  the intensity and count of the different  cells in your in your sample but we're  doing it in situ so directly from the  ffpe slide  so how does this work the approach is  essentially sequential multi-round  immuno labeling so the way that this  works is by taking a tissue section  labeling with three to five antibodies  then we do digital fluorescent slide  scanning after that we strip the  antibodies off the tissue using a  proprietary reagent that we developed  here we repeat this process over and  over until we've covered all the targets  on that same tissue then we use a  computational co-registration algorithm  to assemble this into one big Mega stack  of an image that contains all the  different channels and that allows us to  conduct quantitative analysis where we  do cell counting co-localization  intensity gating Etc  so um this is a brief overview of how  our approach works and some of the  preliminary information to to  demonstrate why we can use it this way  um so the approach that we developed  involves using a chemical technique to  essentially denature the antibodies from  the tissue and this is a very gentle  technique that we've developed  um it's very rapid and this allows us to  do multi-round immunolabling it's  essentially like an antibody eraser  um and so we can take a tissue you can  see on the far left panel we have a  tissue before staining obviously we see  nothing then we stain it with our  selected antibodies we can strip the  tissue which gives us back the tissue as  it was with no signal and now to  demonstrate that we've fully removed all  of the primary and the secondary  antibodies from that tissue we add just  the secondary antibody here to  demonstrate that indeed all the primary  has been removed and we get no  background signal then we can repeat  with new markers in this case we just  demonstrated that the antigenicity is  preserved we can stain with the same  markers and we get in almost  indistinguishable image one of the  coolest advantages of this technique in  the eyes of many Pathologists is that  after you're done collecting all this  fluorescent data you can still go back  and get an h e slide or cross-validate  with conventional immunohistochemistry  techniques and so it makes it very easy  to cross-validate this new technique  with existing with the existing  Pipelines  so this is all the the critical  instrument that allows us to do this is  in our lab is the Imperial Versa and so  this allows us to do Multiplex Channel  multiple Channel slide scanning at 40x  on these samples extremely rapidly  usually takes between 15 minutes to an  hour for these samples depending on the  size we get extremely high image quality  they're extremely well suited for  quantitative analysis and this allows us  to move directly into our next part of  the pipeline  so the most important part of this  process  um at least from the from a data science  standpoint is assembling these images  together into one continuous image if  you're going to do co-localization of  these different biomarkers in the cell  population analysis that's required for  tissue cytometry then we have to make  sure that the different rounds of  Imaging have perfectly aligning cells  the way that we accomplish this is by  using a technique that's known as  computational elastic co-registration  this is a technique that was originally  developed to help  um essentially Stitch satellite images  together way back several decades ago  but has been re-adapted for use in  bioimaging and so we actually used for  for this particular project an image J  plug-in that's publicly available and  free which essentially takes the dappy  channels of the different rounds of  Imaging uses those as a guide and  manages to align these images by  slightly deforming  um The Space Between the cells to  account for the slight deformations that  happen when you remove the coverslip and  repeat the process of staining this  allows us to get one contiguous image  set out of our uh you know out of our  Multiplex image run and that way we can  get a single image with 10 12 15  channels which represent each of the  markers that we're trying to look at in  this panel  so how does this all fit together  so in the next couple slides we'll show  you an example of how we've applied this  technique to take a a look at the  patient at a couple of patients and  understand the differences between the  immune cell population Within These  patients so what we sought to do was  develop a panel of 12 targets um you can  see the targets listed on this slide  here they're mostly for T cells inside  toxic T cells we have some memory detail  markers in here we're looking at  proliferating markers we have pan CK in  there to distinguish between the tumor  regions and the non-tumor stroma regions  as well as some checkpoint markers such  as PD P1 and pdl1 and so we put together  this panel and labeled three different  patients who were all suffering from the  same the same type of breast cancer and  you can see the representative images  here displayed with a zoomed in cutout  down below and as you can see from a  quick glance there's not a huge amount  of difference between these patients  here  um and so  moving along here  um for each of these patients we get a  13 Flex really image stack where we have  12 antibody targets a channel for each  one as well as the nuclear stain  um here's just a representation of that  um the composite image is shown on the  left  um and in the middle you're seeing a  sequential uh essentially video of the  different channels going one after the  other on the same image and off to the  right you can see just a montage of that  sequence  so this enables us to get really  beautiful multi-channel composite images  that look really cool and you know they  go up on the wall but but now what right  this is this doesn't really tell us a  lot  um here we're looking at you know 12  different channels it at the end of the  day there's only so many colors your  eyes can see  um so these images are pretty but they  don't really tell you that much on their  own  um The Next Step of this process  involves computational analysis and so  we utilize image analysis software to do  cell segmentation and marker  co-localization  um so we utilize  um one of several pieces of software we  have several pieces of software we've  developed internally we also use image J  as well as the commercially available  software Halo to process these image  sets depending on the needs from that  image analysis we get huge data so we  get usually more than a million rows per  sample since there's so many cells that  we're detecting in these in these  patient tumors  we take that huge amount of data and we  feed it through some open source data  analysis pipelines that allow us to  generate the conventional Scatter Plots  that that are typically produced when  doing flow cytometry analysis  just to point out the kind of data that  we get for this analysis is also  compatible with flowjo and other  commercially available flow cytometry  software for those who are more  comfortable with it we just wanted to  emphasize that you don't need commercial  software to do this kind of analysis a  very straightforward and can be  reproduced with a basic understanding of  python coding and some of these open  source libraries  so um what is this overall process of of  doing the data analysis for tissue  cytometry  um so first off with the raw data we  filter by size and shape and co-localize  the signals  um then we do some processing such as  background subtraction normalization log  scaling  um and finally we will apply our actual  analysis this is where we're doing the  gating and Counting the statistical  analyzes and one of the advantages that  we have of doing tissue cytometry is we  can do spatial analysis so after we're  done looking at cell populations direct  co-localizations we can dive into which  cell populations were located near one  another in the tissue and that's  critically important for understanding  the roles of the multiple immune cell  phenotypes play in the progression of or  in the progression of  so here's just a representative example  of some selected flow cytometry or sorry  tissue cytometry Scatter Plots here  um and just for a point of uh for of  emphasizing how much data we're really  dealing with when we've got 12 markers  we have 132 one-to-one primary marker  comparisons and if we want to compare  The Coincidence of every marker to every  other marker well that's 12 factorial  possible phenotypes about 500 million  possible combinations to look at if you  want to be complete and so obviously  that's a lot of data so the best way to  do this is is obviously systematically  um and driven by hypotheses and so we  were particularly interested in  understanding the differences in the T  Cell exhaustion between these patients  and so you can see here on the top we  have the Scatter Plots representing the  pd1 intensity is the y-axis and on the  top of the cd3a intensity is the x-axis  and you can see here patient two  displays a particular a particular shift  in its phenotype  um compared to Patient one and we do  that same Trend with patient three  patient one has a significantly higher  relative percentage of CD3 and pd1  positive cells this would be exhausted T  cells compared to Patient two and  patient three  um patient two of patient three we see a  much smaller percentage of the total T  Cell population expressing the pd1  marker this goes hand in hand with what  we see when we look at the pdl1  expression in the tumor and so the  figure on the bottom we see pdl1  intensity in the y-axis and pansy K  intensity on the x-axis and here we see  we have two distinct populations of of  cells in our samples we have a a number  of pdl1 pan CK positive cells and we  also have a number of cells that Express  pdo1 that are not pan CK expressing  um and so you can see when we look at  the bar graph out on right patient one  had a significant a significant decrease  in the I guess significantly fewer pdl1  pan CK negative cells compared to  Patient two or patient 3.  moving along here we were interested in  understanding that tumor Associated  macrophages and so this is where we  could start to look at the advantages of  a spatial analysis and so in our study  we can we can correlate the cd68  positive cells with the nearby pan CK  positive cells and do a scatter plot  here to look at the relative expression  of cd68 in the region where we see panty  K positive cells so you can see at the  figure on the top we have a kind of  bimodal distribution between our three  our three patients we have inpatient one  a significant significantly higher  concentration of our macrophages  contained within the tumor in comparison  to the stroma where a patient two and  patient three show very similar relative  percentage of macrophages in the uh in  the tumor region compared to the stroma  and so to summarize in patient one we're  seeing a significant decrease in the  tumor-associated macrophages in the  stroma compared to patients two and  three  now finally the spatial analysis is what  really brings this tissue cytometry to  the Forefront of interest to Amino  oncology because of the way that we do  this analysis from the actual  immunohisto from the  immunohistofluorescence images we can  take the data analysis and start to  bucket these cells into the distance  from cell types of interest and so we  were particularly 