es per day per scanner that we  have  um we have two verses running pretty  much around the clock here  um but it's a it's a fairly High  throughput technique for for  histopathology  um most importantly in my opinion is the  ability to then do the spatial analysis  and understand the network of immune  cells and how they're interacting and  contributing to changes in the  progression of the of the disease  so there are a few limitations to this  technique so compared to flow cytometry  we have a much lower dynamic range  um the the images typically take 8-bit  images and so we're dealing with you  know between 0 and 255  um as our possible intensity values  um that can make it a little bit  trickier to get separation between your  cell populations unless you're careful  in how you do your data processing  because a lot of tissues have  autofluorescence this requires either  background correction or some kind of  chemical processing ahead of time to  reduce the background  um  course since these tissues are fixed you  cannot isolate the cells easily after  this analysis and because these tissues  have often archived for decades they all  require very careful antigen retrieval  and it's well known that some antigens  are destroyed by ffpe processing so not  every Target will remain available in  archived specimens  so next steps at least on the physical  side we're currently expanding this for  the analysis of tissue microarrays so  that we can offer more complex services  for patient stratification we're next  incorporating a number of panels to  measure aspects about the extracellular  Matrix and tumor microenvironment we're  currently in the process of validating a  wide library of standard panels for  amine markers and other disease markers  and we're continuing to improve our  image analysis techniques by  incorporating AI where it fits best  um so with that I'll draw our  presentation here to a close  um and I'll be happy to take any  questions from the audience and thank  you so much uh for your attention  um  just about do it  thank you Tom for that informative  presentation  just a quick reminder to our audience on  how to submit questions simply type them  into the Q a box and click on the send  button we will answer as many questions  as we have time for  our first question is is this technique  applicable to live cells or fixed cells  that's a great question um so it's not  really applicable to live cells we're  currently validating the stripping  technique and seeing if we can come up  with a with a gentler technique that  wouldn't affect the cell viability so  this is mostly for fixed cells and  tissues it does work well with 3D cell  culture models as well as adherent cells  and so that technique could be applied  in that manner  great thank you  next question can you filter cells by  morphology  oh yeah that's a great question so um  you know since we're doing the image  analysis process we can exclude cells  based on size and shape  um in a similar manner as you can do  with flow cytometry this allows us to  pick out cells that are eliminate cells  that are may be picked up as a mitotic  uh daughter cells or something like that  um so we can add that that layer of  analysis to the uh to the to the  pipeline as well  great next question can confocal images  be used  um yeah actually so we frequently use  this technique on both with our our  versus a slide scanner as well as our  confocal microscope that we have on hand  um it works quite well with confocal in  the same manner um as it does with the  slide scanner  um you can you know use a sample to get  multiple rounds of labeling and do the  same kind of analysis  um even you can get a little bit uh  better results with the confocal since  you get higher resolution  um and a crispr image and so it usually  facilitates the image analysis process  of course the trade-off is it's lower  than the slide scanner  all right thanks next question  um and it looks like 